WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.940
In 2020, Apple announced that they were going
to ditch Intel and use their own chips in

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:05.940
Macs.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.380
This decision effectively broke a 15 year
partnership with Intel and was a major surprise

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.570
for many who weren’t closely following the
industry.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.050
Given that Apple’s unit sales have more
or less stabilized, Apple has been able to

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.699
focus on diversifying their business and bringing
various aspects of their business in house.

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:26.359
And, it looks like their next target will
be even more surprising to many as they are

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:32.110
looking to potentially ditch carriers and
rival SpaceX at the same time.

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:37.020
By now, I think we’ve all heard about Starlink
and what SpaceX is trying to accomplish with

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.020
Starlink.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.760
But if you haven’t, SpaceX is basically
launching thousands of satellites into the

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.610
sky in order to provide fast and reliable
satellite internet to rural places around

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.880
the world.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:52.360
This is a pretty clever idea, as broadband
providers either don’t support rural areas

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.130
or they employ monopolistic pricing schemes
while offering terrible service.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:00.980
So, this market is no doubt ready for disruption
and it’s likely that Starlink will be extremely

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:03.820
successful amongst this population.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.890
Despite these merits though, at the end of
the day, Starlink is only appealing to a niche

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:09.220
demographic.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:13.630
The vast majority of people live in large
cities or in the suburbs of large cities.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:17.860
And, broadband internet providers are quite
competitive within these areas.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.250
Not to mention, many of these areas have fiber
internet which starlink simply can’t compete

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.250
with, at least not today.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:28.631
There is a large market that SpaceX is leaving
on the table though, which is the cell phone

00:01:28.631 --> 00:01:30.180
carrier market.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.540
Fast and reliable satellite internet for cell
phones makes a lot of sense.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.020
I mean, you don’t move your house around
everyday, but you do move your phone around

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.320
all over the place.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:43.720
And, even in the largest cities around the
world, cellular service is often dodgy in

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:44.720
many areas.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.310
So, a satellite internet service for your
cell phone would be quite useful especially

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:51.440
if it increases reliability.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.310
Satellite internet can of course not compete
against the max speeds of 5G.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:02:00.920
But, here’s the thing, 5G is not even that
reliable at this point, and virtually no one

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.200
even needs those speeds on their phones.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.110
When it comes to cell phone service, reliability
is generally much more important than speed,

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:12.970
so if satellite internet is able to drastically
improve reliability for a similar price, it’s

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.170
likely that most will spring for the satellite
internet option.

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.170
Considering this massive opportunity, why
didn’t SpaceX target this market?

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.470
Well, the answer is that they can’t.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:27.970
Satellite internet requires that the receiving
end has a dish or specialized antennas designed

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:29.560
for to receive signals from space.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.980
So, SpaceX needs cooperation from cell phone
makers in order to make satellite cell phone

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.230
service possible.

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:40.080
Apple on the other hand is the cell phone
maker, so they’re able to incorporate satellite

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.500
capability into iPhones if they want.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:45.260
And, this seems to be exactly what they are
doing.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:50.040
In December of 2019, Bloomberg noticed that
Apple was hiring dozens of engineers who specialize

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.319
in satellite and antenna design.

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:57.040
Apparently, this secret team is developing
a method to beam internet directly to iPhones,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.410
iPads, and Macs from space.

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:04.209
This technology does already exist, but satellite
phones are extremely large and bulky.

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:09.099
So, this team is likely focusing on making
this technology compact and seamless.

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:13.739
The bloomberg article also points out that
Tim Cook is behind the effort, so Apple is

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:17.000
likely taking the project extremely seriously.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.380
At the time, Bloomberg expected that if Apple
does end up following through with the project,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:25.880
we’ll likely see a roll out in 5 years meaning
around 2024 or 2025.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.030
Apple doesn’t have launch capability though,
and I don’t think they’re going to develop

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.200
a rocket just to launch their satellites.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.400
If they’re looking for the most economical
way to launch their satellites, it’ll probably

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.989
be through SpaceX.

00:03:36.989 --> 00:03:41.760
So, ironically, SpaceX would be helping Apple
set up their own satellite internet business.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.650
Apple doesn’t have to necessarily use their
own satellites though.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:48.550
They could choose to simply rent out a portion
of the starlink constellation or even partner

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.850
with SpaceX to deploy cell phone specific
satellites.

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:55.540
But given that Apple can easily afford to
create and run their own constellation, I

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.040
don’t think this scenario is as likely.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.760
That’s all cool and all, but how popular
would an Apple cell phone service even be?

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:07.440
After all, Apple is notorious for using kidneys
as their preferred unit of pricing.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.080
Well, while that is true for many of their
top level products, it’s actually not true

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:12.810
at all for their services.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.680
In fact, Apple is extremely competitive when
it comes to their services.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:19.189
Take Apple Music and Spotify for instance.

00:04:19.189 --> 00:04:24.699
They both charge $9.99 a month for their individual
plans, $14.99 a month for their family plans,

00:04:24.699 --> 00:04:27.169
and $4.99 a month for their student plans.

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.319
On top of this, apple music has a more extensive
music library and better integration with

00:04:31.319 --> 00:04:33.919
apple devices for obvious reasons.

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:37.219
This is the same case with iCloud and Google
Drive as well.

00:04:37.219 --> 00:04:42.860
Apple’s cheapest tier offers 50 GB for $0.99
per month while Google’s cheapest tier offers

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:45.939
100 GB for $1.99 per month.

00:04:45.939 --> 00:04:50.479
The price per gigabyte is the same, but Apple
actually does have the cheaper entry price.

00:04:50.479 --> 00:04:55.720
Both companies also offer 200 GB and 1 TB
tiers which are priced identically.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.000
It should be noted that though you can save
16 to 17% by going with Google drive if you

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.690
choose their annual planes.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:06.259
Nonetheless, Apple is quite competitive with
iCloud as well even though they don’t really

00:05:06.259 --> 00:05:07.279
have to be.

00:05:07.279 --> 00:05:11.349
After all, you can’t back up your iPhone
to Google Drive.

00:05:11.349 --> 00:05:14.810
Considering Apple’s history of being competitive
when it comes to services, it’s likely that

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:18.229
they will be competitive within the carrier
market as well.

00:05:18.229 --> 00:05:22.789
With that being said, let’s take a look
at just how profitable this venture can be.

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:27.800
At the start of 2021, Apple announced that
there were officially over 1 billion iphones

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.449
in active use.

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:33.369
Even if Apple was able to capture just 10%
of this market, they would be able to grow

00:05:33.369 --> 00:05:36.430
a subscriber base of over 100 million customers.

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:40.499
And, honestly, this wouldn’t be that hard
for Apple to accomplish given their past success

00:05:40.499 --> 00:05:42.439
with subscription services.

00:05:42.439 --> 00:05:47.469
Apple Music, for instance, had a total of
72 million paid users at the end of 2020.

00:05:47.469 --> 00:05:51.599
But even that is nothing in comparison to
their total paid subscriber base which stood

00:05:51.599 --> 00:05:56.639
at a mind boggling 585 million customers.

00:05:56.639 --> 00:05:58.839
And that was in October of 2020.

00:05:58.839 --> 00:06:02.120
By now, they’re well above the 600 million
customer mark.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:05.599
Also, don’t forget that all of those customers
are buying optional services.

00:06:05.599 --> 00:06:09.300
You don’t actually need apple music or apple
tv or icloud.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.999
But, virtually everyone who buys an iphone
needs a cell phone service.

00:06:12.999 --> 00:06:18.139
And, if satellite based internet is more reliable
and Apple is competitive in terms of pricing,

00:06:18.139 --> 00:06:21.240
choosing Apple wireless or whatever its called
will be a no brainer.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:23.180
I mean just think about it.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:27.520
You’d be getting better wireless service
from Apple themselves for the same price.

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.550
And that’s not even considering the side
benefits.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:34.039
Apple will likely offer bundles with their
other services which may even allow you to

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:36.060
save money by switching to Apple wireless.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:40.539
It’s also likely that many people will switch
simply to get away from legacy carriers who

00:06:40.539 --> 00:06:42.389
people seem to despise.

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:49.000
At&amp;t for instance has a customer rating of
1.2 stars with 91% of customers having a 1

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.159
star experience.

00:06:50.159 --> 00:06:55.569
Similarly, verizon has a customer rating of
1.3 stars with 86% of customers having a 1

00:06:55.569 --> 00:06:56.749
star experience.

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:59.719
Considering this, I don’t think it will
be too terribly difficult for a company like

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:02.919
apple to rack up 100 million subscribers around
the world.

00:07:02.919 --> 00:07:04.801
But, how much money will that even make?

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:09.169
Well, the average American pays $70 per month
for their cell phone service.

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:13.569
If Apple’s average subscriber pays the same
amount, Apple would pull in $7 billion per

00:07:13.569 --> 00:07:17.599
month or $84 billion per year from 100 million
customers.

00:07:17.599 --> 00:07:20.929
Taking a look at Apple’s most recent earnings
report, we’ll see that their gross margin

00:07:20.929 --> 00:07:24.089
on their services is almost exactly 70%.

00:07:24.089 --> 00:07:26.669
But that doesn’t account for operating expenses.

00:07:26.669 --> 00:07:29.999
We aren’t exactly sure what proportion of
their operating expenses goes towards their

00:07:29.999 --> 00:07:33.330
services and what proportion goes towards
their products.

00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:36.430
But if we say that their operating expenses
are proportional to the revenue from each

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.919
sector, we’ll see that about 12% of their
revenue goes towards operating expenses with

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:45.460
6% going towards research and development
and 6% going towards selling, general, and

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:46.949
administrative expenses.

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:52.449
At this point, Apple has 58% of their revenue
remaining out of which 16% will go to taxes

00:07:52.449 --> 00:07:56.309
leaving Apple with about 49% of their revenue
as net profit.

00:07:56.309 --> 00:08:00.360
We want to estimate conservatively though,
so we’ll cut that down substantially and

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.559
call it a net margin of 30%.

00:08:02.559 --> 00:08:07.770
With $84 billion in revenue, we’re left
with $25 billion in net profit annually.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:12.860
To put that in perspective Apple netted about
$75 billion over the past 12 months meaning

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:16.240
that Apple wireless could easily increase
their net income by 33%.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.819
Currently, Apple’s P/E ratio is about 30
which means that the company is worth 30 times

00:08:21.819 --> 00:08:23.389
their annual income.

00:08:23.389 --> 00:08:27.129
The stock market is overextended right now
and Apple’s PE ratio will continue to come

00:08:27.129 --> 00:08:29.909
down as business matures even further.

00:08:29.909 --> 00:08:33.900
So we’ll assume that their PE ratio is down
to 20 by the time they rack up 100 million

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.700
wireless subscribers.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:39.820
Even at that rate, Apple wireless would add
$500 billion onto Apple’s market cap.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:43.510
Where it gets really interesting though is
if Apple is able to get a bigger portion of

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:46.820
the pie which in my opinion is highly likely.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.590
For instance, if Apple had 300 million wireless
subscribers they would pull in $252 billion

00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:55.000
in revenue and $75 billion in net profit.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.910
Even with a PE ratio of 15, Apple wireless
would add over $1.1 trillion onto Apple’s

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.940
already monstrous market cap.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:05.850
At the end of the day, given the shortcomings
of traditional cell phone services, there

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:10.770
is a massive untapped market for satellite
based cell phone service that’s done right.

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.100
Apple is in a unique position where they can
basically grow a monopoly centered around

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.020
providing this service to their billion iPhone
users around the world.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:21.500
By providing this service Apple will not only
be able to grow their bottom line by tens

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:25.510
of billions of dollars and add hundreds of
billions onto their market cap, but they can

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:29.400
also ditch their reliance on carriers and
rival Starlink at the same time.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:32.490
Did you guys realize the massive gold mine
that Apple has been sitting on?

